¶
'''thecosomatous''' (thē-kō-som′a̤-tus), ''a.'' [< NL. ''thecosomatus'', < Gr. θήκη, case, + σῶμα(τ-), body.] Having the body sheathed in a mantle-skirt, as a pteropod; of or pertaining to the ''Thecosomata.''

'''thecosome''' (thē′kō-sōm), ''n.'' A thecosomatous pteropod.

'''thecostomous''' (thē-kos′tō-mus), ''a.'' [< Gr. θήκη, a case, + στόμα, mouth.] In ''entom.'', having the sucking parts of the mouth inclosed in a sheath.

'''thedam†, thedom†, thedome†,''' ''n.'' Same as ''theedom.''

'''thee<sup>1</sup>†''' (thē), ''v. i.'' [< ME. ''theen'', ''then'', or without the inf. suffix ''thee'', ''the'', < AS. ''theón'', ''thión'', ''ge-theón'', be strong, thrive, = OS. *''thīhan'', found only in the derived factitive ''thengian'', complete, = D. ''gedijen'', thrive, prosper, succeed, = OHG. ''gidīhan'', MHG. ''gedīhen'', G. ''gedeihen'' = Goth. ''gatheihan'', increase, thrive; orig., as the old participial form AS. ''ge-thungen'' shows, with a nasal suppressed (as usual before ''h''), AS. *''thinhan:'' cf. Lith. ''tenku'', ''tekti'', have enough; Ir. ''tocad'', W. ''tynged'', luck, fortune.] To thrive; prosper.

{{smaller block|To traysen her that trewe is unto me,<br>I pray God let this counseyl never ''the.''
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, iv. 439.}}}}

{{smaller block|Quod Coueitise "And alle folk were trewe,<br>Manye a man schulde neuere ''thee.''"
{{right|''Hymns to Virgin'', etc. (E. E. T. S.), p. 63.}}}}

{{smaller block|[Especially common in the phrase ''also'' or ''so mote I thee'', so may I prosper.}}

{{smaller block|Lasse harm is, ''so mote I the'',<br>Deceyve hem, than deceyved be.
{{right|''Rom. of the Rose'', l. 4841.}}}}

{{smaller block|The form ''theech'', from ''thee ich'', is also found in the phrase ''so theech'', so may I thrive; also ''so theek.''}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|He who, still wanting, though he lives on ''theft'',
Steals much, spends little, yet has nothing left.
{{right|''Pope'', Prol. to Satires, l. 183.}}}}

{{smaller block|The term ''theft'' in modern English law is sometimes used as a synonym of larceny, sometimes in a more comprehensive sense.
{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', XXIII. 232.}}}}

2. Something stolen; a loss by stealing.

{{smaller block|If the ''theft'' be certainly found in his hand alive, whether it be ox, or ass, or sheep, he shall restore double.
{{right|Ex. xxii. 4.}}}}

{{smaller block|If he steal aught the whilst this play is playing,
And 'scape detecting, I will pay the ''theft''.
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, iii. 2. 94.}}}}

'''Reset of theft.''' {{smaller|See ''reset''<sup>1</sup>.}}

'''theft-boot†''' (theft′böt), ''n.'' [Also ''theft-bote'', Sc. ''thiftbote''; < ''theft'' + ''boot''<sup>1</sup>.] In ''law'', the receiving of one's goods again from a thief, or a compensation for them by way of composition, upon an agreement not to prosecute: a form of compounding felony.

{{smaller block|We hae aneugh, and it looks unco like ''theft-boot'', or hush-money, as they ca' it.
{{right|''Scott'', Heart of Mid-Lothian, xlviii.}}}}

'''theftuous''' (thef′tū-us), ''a.'' [Formerly also ''thiefteous'', ''thefteous'', Sc. also ''thifteous'', ''thiftous''; < ''theft'' + ''-u-ous''.] Of the nature of theft; thievish. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|Was not the ''thefteous'' stealing away of the daughter from her own father the first ground whereupon all this great noise hath since proceeded?
{{right|''King James I.'', To Bacon, Aug. 23, 1617.}}}}
